# ── Copy to .env and edit. Everything the deploy needs lives here. ─────────────
# ./deploy/install.sh generates most of the secrets below for you; this file documents what
# every setting means and is what a hand-edit or a --dry-run diffs against.

# Postgres (stays on the box, bound to 127.0.0.1)
POSTGRES_USER=lumberroom
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME_openssl_rand_hex_24
POSTGRES_DB=lumberroom
POSTGRES_PORT=5432

# Server
SERVER_PORT=8787
TENANT_ID=me
LOG_LEVEL=info

# The public origin this server is reached at, no trailing slash. Every externally visible URL
# (the MCP endpoint, the OAuth issuer, the metadata documents, redirect targets) is derived from
# this one setting. Required, and must be https://, for AUTH_MODE=oauth. install.sh sets it from
# --domain.
PUBLIC_URL=https://memory.example.com

# ── Auth ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# token: shared bearer tokens from AUTH_TOKENS. Static tokens are honoured in every mode below,
#        so the CLI and hook-based clients keep working once a browser mode is turned on.
# oauth: the built-in authorization server. Browser and mobile clients (Claude.ai, Cowork,
#        ChatGPT) need this; a static header is not accepted by any of them. See deploy/oauth.md.
# oidc:  validate JWTs issued by an external issuer such as Logto (see deploy/logto.md).
AUTH_MODE=token

# One entry per client. Generate each token with: openssl rand -hex 32
#
# The value below is the owner's own client: every namespace, up to `sealed`. Anything lower and the
# first write into personal:finance is refused, since that namespace classifies `private` by default
# (see the classification table further down). Each entry in a grant list carries a namespace glob
# and a sensitivity ceiling, which are the two axes the server enforces.
#
# Three ways to write a grant list, and they do not mean the same thing:
#   {"namespace":"*","max":"sealed"}  the object form. The ceiling is the one you typed.
#   "*"                               a bare string. The ceiling is `open`, on purpose: a grant
#                                     written before the axis existed gains no reach over content
#                                     that did not exist when it was written.
#   read or write omitted             unrestricted at every level, which is how a token issued
#                                     before any of this kept working.
# So the compact form at the bottom of this comment reaches further than a JSON grant that says
# "read":["*"]. Type the object form and the question never comes up.
#
# registryWrite is off unless stated. The registry holds credential locations, so writing to it is
# an operator action; give it to the client you run `lumberroom registry set` from and to nothing else.
# An explicitly empty read or write list means no access.
#
# sealedCapable asserts the client can decrypt locally. A client without it can hold a sealed
# ceiling and still only ever receive ciphertext.
#
# mayDelete is off, for the owner's own client too, and that is a decision rather than an omission.
# A model that can silently delete memories is a worse failure than one that hoards them, and
# memory_forget stays out of tools/list for a client without the flag, so nothing puts the idea in
# front of a model in the first place.
#
# The flag gates every delete path, `lumberroom forget` included: the difference between a CLI call and a
# tool call is a request header the caller picks, and src/services/forget.rs refuses to treat a
# header as an authorization boundary. So with the grant below, nothing deletes, and you get a
# refusal naming this setting rather than a surprise. Two ways to change that, whenever you want it:
#   - set mayDelete true here, which also hands memory_forget to the model on this token;
#   - or add a second entry the model never holds, object-form grants at sealed plus
#     "mayDelete":true, client `lumberroom`, and run the CLI against it with LUMBERROOM_TOKEN.
# The second keeps delete away from every model, at the cost of one more token to look after.
#
# mayIngest is off the same way, for the owner's own client too, and no client has it unless the
# entry says so. It gates every /admin/ingest route: opening a run, posting proposals, approving
# them and moving a watermark. A client that can post proposals can fill the queue, and a queue
# nobody finishes reading is an approval gate in name only. The header that tells `lumberroom ingest`
# apart from a model is one a model can set for free, so the grant is the boundary. Set
# "mayIngest":true on the entry you run ingestion from, and on nothing else; a client without it
# gets a 403 naming the flag.
#
# The JSON form MUST be single-quoted. This file is sourced by `sh` in more than one script
# (deploy/backup.sh, for one), and sourcing an unquoted value that contains double quotes strips
# them: "client" becomes client, the JSON becomes invalid, and ["*"] becomes a glob `sh` tries to
# expand. Docker Compose parses .env itself and strips a single layer of quoting around the whole
# value, so single-quoting is the one form both readers agree on. Nobody noticed this for months
# because only the sh-sourcing scripts were affected, not compose. Do not remove the quotes.
#
# The compact form omits both lists, so it grants every namespace at every level. Use it for a
# client you would have written `*` at `sealed` for anyway:
#   AUTH_TOKENS=claude-code-mac:<token>,browser:<token>
AUTH_TOKENS='[{"client":"claude-code-mac","token":"CHANGE_ME_openssl_rand_hex_32","read":[{"namespace":"*","max":"sealed"}],"write":[{"namespace":"*","max":"sealed"}],"sealedCapable":true,"mayDelete":false,"mayIngest":false,"registryWrite":true}]'

# Kept for compatibility with tooling that still reads it directly; PUBLIC_URL is authoritative
# when both are set. install.sh does not write this anymore.
MCP_RESOURCE_URL=

# ── AUTH_MODE=oauth (the built-in authorization server) ────────────────────────
# Argon2id hash of the owner's password. Never store the password itself. install.sh prompts for
# the password and runs this for you rather than letting the server crash at boot on a missing
# hash; by hand, mask the read so the password does not land in shell history or a scrollback:
#   read -rs OWNER_PW; echo; printf '%s' "$OWNER_PW" | docker compose run --rm -T server lumberroom-server hash-password; unset OWNER_PW
# See deploy/oauth.md.
OWNER_PASSWORD_HASH=
# HMAC key for the consent-screen cookie, at least 32 characters. Generate with:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
OAUTH_COOKIE_SECRET=
# RFC 7591 self-registration. On by default: registration alone holds nothing until the owner
# logs in and consents. Turn off to require every client to be issued by hand.
OAUTH_DCR_ENABLED=true
# Registrations each caller may make per minute before /oauth/register answers 429. Keyed like the
# login throttle, so one registration storm cannot lock the owner out of the login form.
OAUTH_REGISTRATIONS_PER_MINUTE=5
OAUTH_CODE_TTL_SECS=120
OAUTH_ACCESS_TTL_SECS=3600
OAUTH_REFRESH_TTL_SECS=5184000
OAUTH_SESSION_TTL_SECS=900
# Preselected on the consent screen; the owner still chooses. See deploy/oauth.md for what the
# grant profiles mean.
OAUTH_DEFAULT_PROFILE=standard
OAUTH_SCOPES=memory.read,memory.write
OAUTH_LOGIN_ATTEMPTS_PER_MINUTE=5
# The token endpoint records on every access token the resource it was minted for (RFC 8707). This
# setting decides what the server does with that record when the token comes back.
#
# off      compare nothing, at the token endpoint and at the authenticator alike. A token minted
#          for one resource authenticates at any other, and a client may ask for a resource this
#          deployment does not serve. What every build before this one did.
# lenient  a token that names a resource must name the one this deployment serves. A token that
#          names none is admitted. Default.
# strict   the same match, and a token that names no resource is refused.
#
# This covers the tokens the built-in authorization server issues and nothing else. An AUTH_TOKENS
# entry and an external issuer's JWT reach a Principal by other paths, so strict does not tighten
# them; their fences are the grant list above and OIDC_AUDIENCE.
#
# The default is lenient rather than strict because every token issued before this landed carries
# no resource at all, so strict on the day of the deploy refuses every live client at once. Once
# the deployment runs this build the token endpoint stamps its own resource on anything the client
# did not name, and the tokens carrying none drain within one OAUTH_ACCESS_TTL_SECS. Flip to strict
# after that window has passed. A client that asks for a resource this deployment does not serve is
# refused at the token endpoint with RFC 8707's `invalid_target`, whichever value you set here.
#
# `off` is for a deployment reached under two hostnames whose clients discover different resource
# URLs. There the match refuses a caller that did nothing wrong, and the refusal is the bug.
OAUTH_RESOURCE_AUDIENCE=lenient

# ── AUTH_MODE=oidc (Logto or another external issuer) ───────────────────────────
OIDC_ISSUER=
OIDC_AUDIENCE=
OIDC_JWKS_URI=
# The accounts allowed in, by the `sub` claim of their tokens, comma separated. Required in oidc
# mode, and an empty list is a boot error rather than a wildcard. Grants are keyed on the client
# id; that id sits in every client's config file and in the address bar of every sign-in, and the
# issuer signs a token for whoever signs up. Without this list an account a stranger created
# presents a token that validates and picks up your grant. Read the value off the issuer's user
# detail page, or decode the `sub` of a token it already issued you.
OIDC_ALLOWED_SUBJECTS=
# A scope your API resource defines, granted through a role only you hold. Also required in oidc
# mode, as a second fence rather than the control: a scope carried by the issuer's default role
# admits the same stranger, which is why OIDC_ALLOWED_SUBJECTS is what decides.
OIDC_REQUIRED_SCOPES=

# ── Embeddings ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# local  bge-base-en-v1.5, the most-quantized variant fastembed ships for it, baked into the
#        image so a locked-down box never downloads at first request. Default.
# hash   deterministic, no model. Tests and emergencies only; readiness reports 503 when in use.
# openai any OpenAI-compatible /embeddings endpoint (Workers AI, OpenRouter, llama-server, TEI).
#        EMBED_MODEL is the name sent to it. A model narrower than EMBED_DIM is zero-padded.
EMBED_PROVIDER=local
EMBED_MODEL=Xenova/bge-base-en-v1.5
EMBED_DIM=768
EMBED_ALLOW_FALLBACK=false
# openai only. The endpoint up to and including /v1, and its key if it wants one.
EMBED_BASE_URL=
EMBED_API_KEY=
# openai only. Text prepended to queries and to stored documents. Unset, they follow the model
# name: embeddinggemma and bge-*-en get the prefixes they were trained with, anything else none.
EMBED_QUERY_PREFIX=
EMBED_DOCUMENT_PREFIX=
# openai only. A character cap on each input; 0 sends it whole. llama-server refuses an input past
# its window instead of truncating, so set this near three characters per token of that window.
EMBED_MAX_INPUT_CHARS=0
EMBED_TIMEOUT_SECS=60

# ── Sensitivity and policy (Phase 3) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# The classification table: namespace=level pairs, longest matching pattern first. A write with no
# explicit level takes its namespace default, so nobody classifies anything in the normal case.
#
# Two ways to set it, and the precedence between them is fixed:
#   1. this variable, which wins whenever it carries rules, because setting it means it;
#   2. the `sensitivity_default` table, which migration 004 seeds with exactly the rules below and
#      which you edit with SQL rather than a redeploy:
#        INSERT INTO sensitivity_default (tenant_id, pattern, sensitivity)
#        VALUES ('me', 'personal:*', 'private')
#        ON CONFLICT (tenant_id, pattern) DO UPDATE SET sensitivity = EXCLUDED.sensitivity;
# Leave this blank to use the table. Blank is not "no rules": an empty rule set would classify
# everything open, so the server falls through to the table and then to these same seeded rules, and
# logs one `classification table` line at boot naming which of the three it ended up on.
#
# `credentials:*` at sealed is load-bearing. memory_write refuses those namespaces outright; the
# ciphertext gets there through `lumberroom seal`, which encrypts on the client.
SENSITIVITY_DEFAULTS=*=open,global=open,project:*=open,user:me=open,personal:finance=private,personal:health=private,credentials:*=sealed
# The credential tripwire: refuses a write of credential-shaped content at `open`.
SENSITIVITY_TRIPWIRE=true
# Highest level memory_write accepts from a tool argument. Sealed content is encrypted
# client-side and never arrives as plaintext, so this cannot be set to sealed.
MAX_WRITE_SENSITIVITY=private

# ── Key-encryption key, for `private` rows ───────────────────────────────────
# personal:finance and personal:health classify `private` by default, so a deployment with no key
# has a documented sensitivity axis it cannot use: every write into those namespaces is refused.
# `file` is what this file ships, and install.sh provisions it.
#
# file  read a 64-hex or 44-base64 char key from KEK_PATH. install.sh generates ./secrets/lumberroom-kek,
#       chowns it to the container's uid:gid (10001:10001) and chmods it 600; docker-compose.yml
#       bind-mounts it read-only at KEK_PATH. Deliberately not a Docker Compose `secrets:` entry:
#       Compose honours per-secret uid/gid/mode under Swarm only, and under plain
#       `docker compose up` a secret always lands owned by root, mode 0444, which the server
#       refuses to read (kek.rs rejects a key file open to group or other). That path would fail
#       at boot every time.
# env   read the key from KEK_ENV_VAR. Convenient, weaker: the environment of a container is
#       readable by anything that can inspect it (docker inspect, /proc, a core dump).
# none  refuses private writes rather than storing them in plaintext. The refusal is the point and
#       it stays whatever else changes: no key configured never means a private fact in the clear.
#       Set this when you would rather have no private content at all than a key to look after.
#
# By hand, without install.sh, three commands. Create the file before compose sees it: a missing
# bind-mount source makes Docker create a directory there, and the server then fails to read its
# own key.
#   mkdir -p secrets && touch secrets/lumberroom-kek && chmod 600 secrets/lumberroom-kek
#   docker compose run --rm --no-deps -T server lumberroom-server generate-kek > secrets/lumberroom-kek
#   sudo chown 10001:10001 secrets/lumberroom-kek     # the uid:gid the Dockerfile pins for `lumberroom`
# Then `docker compose up -d server` and check `curl -s 127.0.0.1:8787/readyz`: kek_provider is
# "file" and kek_verified is true. The first boot with a key records its fingerprint in
# `kek_state`; a later boot with a different key reports a mismatch and keeps refusing private
# writes rather than writing rows nobody can read.
#
# Back that file up somewhere this box does not hold, and keep it out of the database backups.
# Losing it makes every private row permanently unreadable, dumps included, because each row's
# data key is wrapped under it. Nobody has decided where the offline copy lives yet
# (docs/decisions/0004-kek-provider.md leaves escrow open); decide it before there is private
# content worth losing.
KEK_PROVIDER=file
KEK_PATH=/run/secrets/lumberroom-kek
KEK_ENV_VAR=LUMBERROOM_KEK
# Only read when KEK_PROVIDER=env. Never commit a real value here.
LUMBERROOM_KEK=
KEK_ID=kek-1
# Refuse to write an encrypted row until a boot has proved the key can be recovered.
REQUIRE_VERIFIED_KEK=true

# ── Quality (Phase 4) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DEDUPE_THRESHOLD=0.97
CONFLICT_THRESHOLD=0.90
CONFLICT_LIMIT=3
STALE_DAYS=365
# Highest sensitivity the Obsidian export may include.
EXPORT_MAX_SENSITIVITY=open
# What one uploaded archive may decompress to before the reader refuses it. A compressed file
# expands inside this process before anything parses it, so this is the number that decides whether
# a hostile upload is an error or an outage. 2 GiB.
ARCHIVE_MAX_DECOMPRESSED_BYTES=2147483648

# ── TLS (compose profile: tls) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Caddy reads LUMBERROOM_DOMAIN for the certificate it issues. `install.sh --behind-proxy` sets it
# too, with Caddy switched off, and there it only feeds PUBLIC_URL.
LUMBERROOM_DOMAIN=memory.example.com
ACME_EMAIL=you@example.com

# ── Logto (compose profile: logto, AUTH_MODE=oidc) ──────────────────────────────
LOGTO_DOMAIN=auth.example.com
LOGTO_ADMIN_DOMAIN=auth-admin.example.com

# ── Backups (deploy/backup.sh, not read by the server) ──────────────────────────
# deploy/backup.sh refuses to write a plaintext dump unless BACKUP_ALLOW_PLAINTEXT=true. Generate
# a dedicated age keypair, NOT the KEK (see the comment at the top of deploy/backup.sh for why):
#   age-keygen -o backup-key.txt
# and put the public half here. Keep backup-key.txt off this box; it is only needed to restore.
BACKUP_AGE_RECIPIENT=
# Alternative to the above: a path to an age -R recipients file, one public key per line.
BACKUP_AGE_RECIPIENTS_FILE=
# Local/dev only. A box holding real data should never set this.
BACKUP_ALLOW_PLAINTEXT=false

# ── Tuning (safe defaults; override only with a reason) ───────────────────────
BOOTSTRAP_CACHE_MS=30000
# Per-client digest budgets, since response ceilings differ by surface and one is undocumented
# (docs/specs/phase-2-surfaces.md §4a). client=chars pairs or JSON, for example:
#   BOOTSTRAP_MAX_CHARS_BY_CLIENT=chatgpt=3000,claude-code-mac=20000
BOOTSTRAP_MAX_CHARS_BY_CLIENT=
# Rows per digest section, newest first. Profile reads user:me and global, project the active one.
BOOTSTRAP_PROFILE_LIMIT=10
BOOTSTRAP_PROJECT_LIMIT=5
# Of two digest rows at or above this cosine, the older is dropped as a restatement and the next
# row takes its slot. Above 0 and at most 1; 1 turns it off. docs/decisions/0025.
BOOTSTRAP_DEDUP_COSINE=0.90
SEARCH_DEFAULT_LIMIT=8
# How the two search arms combine. `linear` adds their raw scores, which is what shipped.
# `rrf` fuses their ranks, where both arms carry comparable weight by construction.
# `linear_minmax` rescales each query's candidate cosines to 0..1, then adds as `linear` does.
SEARCH_FUSION=
SEARCH_RRF_K=
# true attaches each hit's cosine, keyword score and fused score to memory_search answers. For a
# retrieval harness on a scratch server; the ranking is the same either way. Empty means false.
SEARCH_DEBUG_SCORES=
SEARCH_USAGE_WEIGHT=0.05
# Weight of the full-text arm, 0.35 when empty. 0 turns that arm off and search runs on vectors
# alone, skipping the full-text scan. Set 0 for a store written in Chinese, Japanese or Thai: the
# full-text parser splits on spaces, so a sentence in those scripts is one token and no query word
# matches it. Hindi, Hinglish and English match word by word and need no change.
SEARCH_LEXICAL_WEIGHT=

# Postgres pool. Both default to the values that were hard-coded before: 10 connections, 5 seconds.
# Searches, writes and the background touch and recall tasks share the pool, so a busy server may
# want more than 10. Boot refuses a size you set here that is above the server's max_connections
# minus its superuser_reserved_connections minus 3 spare. Left unset, the default 10 only logs a
# warning when the server is that small, so an upgrade never fails
# to boot. Uncommenting the line makes the size explicit, and an explicit size that does not
# fit is refused. A request that waits longer than the acquire timeout for a free connection fails
# as unavailable. Both must be at least 1.
# DB_MAX_CONNECTIONS=10
# DB_ACQUIRE_TIMEOUT_SECS=5

# The deterministic cleanup pass, inside the server. Seconds between runs; 0 turns it off, and that
# is the only way to turn it off. The model pass is not on this timer and cannot be: it calls a
# provider and this process holds the KEK. See docs/cleanup-schedule.md.
CLEANUP_INTERVAL_SECS=3600
# A namespace glob to narrow the pass to. Absent means every namespace, which is the safe default:
# a pass that covers part of the store reports a clean bill of health for the part it never read.
# CLEANUP_NAMESPACE=project:*
CLEANUP_LIMIT=500

# The per-call recall log: one row per memory each memory_search or context_bootstrap returned, with
# the call id, rank and digest section and no content. For offline evaluation of ranking; nothing in
# the server reads it. Off by default. The cleanup pass above deletes rows older than the retention
# window, so boot refuses RECALL_EVENT_LOG=true with CLEANUP_INTERVAL_SECS=0. Retention is at least 1
# day. An event can live up to the retention plus one CLEANUP_INTERVAL_SECS, because the pass skips
# its first tick after boot. With the log off and CLEANUP_INTERVAL_SECS=0, old rows never age out.
# Dumps from deploy/backup.sh keep the events they hold for BACKUP_RETAIN_DAYS. See
# docs/recall-event-log.md.
RECALL_EVENT_LOG=false
RECALL_EVENT_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# The model half, as its own container: docker compose --profile cleanup up -d
# A client token carrying mayIngest. Without it the daemon refuses at startup rather than at 04:25.
# install.sh mints the matching AUTH_TOKENS entry as
# {"client":"cleanup","token":"...","read":[{"namespace":"*","max":"open"}],"write":[],"mayIngest":true}
# scoped to read at `open` everywhere, with no write grant, no sealedCapable, no registryWrite, no
# mayDelete. Every pair the daemon is handed goes to a provider and the run withholds anything
# above open, so a higher ceiling buys it nothing; the in-server pass (CLEANUP_INTERVAL_SECS) is
# what groups private duplicates. Leaving read or write off the entry means unrestricted (see the
# AUTH_TOKENS comment above), which for this token is every namespace at `sealed`; type the object
# form if you add this by hand instead of through install.sh.
LUMBERROOM_CLEANUP_TOKEN=
CLEANUP_DAILY_AT=04:25
CLEANUP_PROVIDER=openrouter
CLEANUP_MODEL=qwen/qwen3.7-flash
CLEANUP_MIN_SIMILARITY=0.65
